"The prognosis is influenced by several documented factors. Favorable findings include a low preoperative PSA of 2.11, a low surgical grade of Gleason 3+3 (ISUP grade group 1), negative surgical margins (0.0), and no invasion of the seminal vesicles (0.0). The absence of lymphovascular invasion and the lack of documented extraprostatic extension are also positive prognostic indicators. Countervailing factors include the lack of documented nodal status, as no lymph nodes were removed, which widens uncertainty, and the missing Gleason pattern and ISUP report from the biopsy session. The uncertainty regarding the postoperative PSA and its subsequent trajectory is a significant factor affecting the final assessment. Missing nodal sampling further complicates the certainty of the outcome. Information regarding postoperative PSA levels and their trajectory would be crucial in revising this prognosis. The fixed estimated horizon is 93.67 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 60.89–126.46 months; its coverage has not been validated."
